{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 4000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the first asteroids were discovered over 200 \nyears ago, they were celebrated as new planets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8880,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the story goes that the rapid and overwhelming \ndiscovery of hundreds and then thousands more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly convinced scientists that asteroids \nshouldn't be planets - but that’s totally wrong:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the real reason asteroids were demoted from \n“planet” status came nearly 100 years after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hundredth asteroid was discovered and decades \nafter the thousandth, and had nothing to do with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their quantity, but in fact, their quality.\nOf course, up until 1800, only seven primary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30080,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets – objects that directly orbit the Sun – \nonly seven primary planets had been discovered:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34000,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn \nand Uranus. But telescopes got better and we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found more: a small 8th primary planet, Ceres, \nwas discovered in 1801 between Jupiter and Mars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43120,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then there was a surge of new planets \ndiscovered with almost identical orbits to Ceres:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pallas, Juno, Vesta, and more, and more, and \nmore! It soon became clear that there was a band  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51760,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of small planets orbiting the Sun between Mars and \nJupiter, and so, when describing the solar system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronomers found it useful to group them together \nas “the small planets”, “ the minor planets”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59600,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“the planetoids”, “the asteroids” or “the \nnumbered planets”, because eventually so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63200,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many were being discovered so quickly it was \neasier to keep track of them all right away with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65920,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers rather than coming up with fancy names.\nBut there wasn’t yet any scientific reason to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stop thinking of the asteroids as planets. \nI mean, “having too many” isn’t by itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73920,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a good scientific criteria (think about all the \nthousands of known planets we’ve now discovered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77360,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbiting other stars – their sheer number isn’t \na reason not to call them planets!). So despite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some embarrassment over the rapidly growing list \nof planets 19th and early 20th century astronomers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89920,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still considered all the asteroids to be planets. \nWell, except one astronomer, who argued that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93760,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asteroids should be their own category, but \nhis reasoning was based on incorrect data and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theoretical misunderstandings and was ultimately \nrejected by the wider astronomical communidy.   "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even today, the International Astronomical Union’s \ncommission on asteroids is STILL technically named  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the “Minor Planet Center” (even though, as we are \nabout to discover, it’s a poor name for them). "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109760,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why don’t we include the asteroids in our \nlist of planets today? Look at this analysis  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of scientific papers in the last 200 years: the \nterms “small” and “minor” planets had consistent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "use relative to “asteroid” right up until \n1953, when, boom! their use plummeted. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "change wasn’t because of a fear of a huge list \nof planets, nor from a vote by a bureaucracy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126640,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead from a scientific discovery: \nAsteroids, it turns out, are not just miniature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "version of the larger planets, but are in fact the \nfragments from the collisions and disintegration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134640,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of fully formed planets (or protoplanets), rather \nthan miniature versions of the larger planets. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135600,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This distinction matters because when planets \nform, the release of gravitational potential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy partially melts their interiors, causing \nheavy elements to sink to the core and lighter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "materials to float upwards, sorting into \nlayers. If asteroids were formed like planets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147120,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but smaller, they would each be made of a mix \nof materials, and would be too small to have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150560,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough energy to sort the materials into layers. \nIf instead asteroids were relics from collisions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between planets, each one would be a mostly \nhomogenous chunk of material from a single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer. And what astronomers figured out was that \nasteroids are definitely broken planet pieces - in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162800,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact, asteroids are literally classified into \ngroups based on what materials they’re made of. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166400,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Out of the ridiculous number of objects in the \nasteroid belt, there are only three or four that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170160,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we think formed on their own as a planet \nrather than being broken planet fragments:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these three or four are the biggest: \nCeres, Vesta, Pallas, and maybe Hygia. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So. The asteroids were demoted to \nnon-planet status, not by a vote,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nor from a dislike of a long list of planets, but \ndue to a scientific discovery that asteroids are,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186560,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fact, physically different from everything else \nthat we call planets. The discovery meant they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190240,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were split off (in the minds of scientists) \ninto their own group, and those scientists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193680,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organically stopped calling them planets. In \nprinciple, the taxonomic split could have gone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197440,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other way – researchers could have kept \ncalling asteroids planets, and come up with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new name for the remaining non-fragmented objects \n- but given the fact that asteroids were already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previously considered a subcategory of planet \n(minor planets), it makes much more taxonomic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense to split them off and keep all the rest of \nthe planets, as planets. By this logic, we should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also probably stop calling Ceres, Vesta and Pallas \nasteroids since they formed like planets do. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218560,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, even though there are lots of asteroids, \nfor the question of whether or not asteroids  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "count as planets, it turns out that \nquality matters more than quantity. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227360,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seeing the innumerable fascinating - but \ncompletely inhospitable - worlds in our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar system is a reminder of just how diverse, \nunique and delicate our home planet is. But to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235280,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep planet Earth as our home, we have to \nlook after it. That’s why we working with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238480,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nature-protection community called Planet \nWild, our sponser for this video. Every month,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet wild community funds a mission to \nrestore the oceans, forests and life here on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246320,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet Earth. All of this is documented through \nvideos here on YouTube, just like this mission  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restoring lost peatland ecosystems. You can join \nin supporting solution-oriented missions globally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254560,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by giving whatever amount you want. The first 100 \npeople to sign up using our code MINUTEPHYICS1  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258720,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will get their first month covered. Just scan \nthis QR code or click the link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
